all over the planet.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:02.490
We've tried to be as
complete as possible,

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:05.670
covering the oceans
and the land masses.

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:08.920
And we invest a lot in
keeping that data fresh.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:13.690
So we're doing like 25 million
updates to our data a day.

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:17.130
And that's updating what
business is at what location

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:18.930
or what's changed in
the roads, or there's

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:24.210
been some flooding-- all of that
from just very tabular textual

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:28.920
data about listings, but
also visual data imagery.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:33.030
We've seen that we have
satellite imagery for overhead.

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:36.350
We have aerial imagery for
more of the low altitude,

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.700
like 45 degree
angles of buildings.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.140
And we have Street View.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.780
You've seen the famous Street
View cars driving around

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.690
on the streets capturing,
really, at that address level

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:48.330
and at that building level,
what you see on the streets.

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.090
And something I didn't know
before I joined Google Maps was

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:52.890
not only are there
Street View vehicles,

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:55.980
but there are Street View
backpacks for hikers to take.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:59.040
You can see this site called
Street View Treks that you

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.790
can see really amazing views
of the world of areas that

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:07.670
are really hard to get to, like
the front face in Yosemite of--

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:08.987
what's that big rock?

00:09:08.987 --> 00:09:10.320
MARK MANDEL: There's a big rock?

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.095
I don't know.

00:09:11.095 --> 00:09:12.300
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There's
a lot of big rocks.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:12.990
MARK MANDEL: There are.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:13.657
ANGELA YU: Yeah.

00:09:13.657 --> 00:09:15.390
Yeah, like that,
and the Himalayas.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:17.550
And basically, you
can take a backpack.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:18.990
You can take a tricycle.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:19.530
We've--

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:20.460
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Is it El Capitan?

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:20.750
ANGELA YU: El Capitan.

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:21.210
[DING]

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:21.288
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There we go.

00:09:21.288 --> 00:09:22.121
I just looked it up.

00:09:22.121 --> 00:09:25.680
MAN: Soaring over
3,590 feet, El Capitan

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.810
is bucket list destination
overlooking Yosemite National

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:29.310
Park.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:31.710
ANGELA YU: Clearly,
I'm not a hiker.

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:35.610
But we've enabled bringing that
Street View 360 degree imagery

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.610
experience out to the most
remote corners of the world

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:41.310
in order to give you any kind
of window you want to see.

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:42.060
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:45.780
Well, as someone who grew up
with a physically bound paper

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:47.390
map that made me drive around--

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:48.360
ANGELA YU: The paper maps, yeah.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:49.380
MARK MANDEL: We had one
called the Melways, because I

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:50.640
lived in Melbourne, Australia.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:51.640
It's called the Melways.

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:54.330
Thank you so much for
getting rid of that for us.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:55.080
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah,
I think a lot of people

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:57.000
have used Google Maps,
I mean, hopefully

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.430
to kind of replace some of
the older mapping stuff.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:00.180
We have this live.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.680
I mean, like you
were saying, you're

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.400
pulling in a tremendous
amount of data.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:05.880
But there's also a Google
Maps API for other people

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.290
who want to kind of pull
in their own data, right?

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:08.248
ANGELA YU: That's true.

00:10:08.248 --> 00:10:11.340
Yes, so the API allows you
to bring all of that data

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.800
that we've collected
into your own apps.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:16.590
Because everybody has their
own view of the world.

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:19.620
It's not just what is out
there and what is public.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:22.095
But you might care
about where are you

00:10:22.095 --> 00:10:23.970
and where are the things
that you care about,

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:25.830
whether that's from
a personal level,

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.050
like the people in
your life, or if you

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:30.870
track your personal property,
if it's moving around.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:33.000
Or if you're a business
and organization,

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.190
and you want to track your
physical assets out there

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:35.890
in the world.

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:36.840
MARK MANDEL: So
what sort of things

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.790
are people doing with
the Google Maps API?

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:40.690
What are some good examples?

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:43.190
ANGELA YU: The really cool thing
about working on the Google

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:46.500
Maps API is it spans everyone,
from the individual hobbyist

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.690
developer to the
largest enterprises.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.120
Because everybody
has some reason

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.250
to be interested in location.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:56.010
Google Maps API is kind
of often the first Google

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:57.960
API that a developer
will use, just

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.140
because as we're experimenting,
we're learning new apps,

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:04.110
it's really satisfying
to pull in a map

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:06.160
and have that show
up on your app,

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:07.690
whether it's on
the web or mobile.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.540
And it's one of the
simplest APIs to use.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:12.810
So in the beginning,
individual developers

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:15.180
can start using
location data just

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.070
to show things like
where is my user,

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:18.870
and show you that geolocation.

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:21.840
And then you can
broaden that to if you

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:25.860
are an app that wants to
show the user certain things

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:27.507
around them that
is not generally

00:11:27.507 --> 00:11:29.340
what the regular Google
Maps app would show,

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:30.780
which would be a
lot of businesses.

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:32.947
You might want to show them
specific things, like EV

00:11:32.947 --> 00:11:36.750
charging stations or things
that are specific to what

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:38.400
the purpose of your app is.

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:41.820
That is really useful to
both tap into our database

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.250
and/or maybe you have a
custom database of your own

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:46.622
of where things are
geolocated that you

00:11:46.622 --> 00:11:47.830
want to show around the user.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:51.160
And also for all
different kinds of users,

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:55.930
you can use the information of
what is around the environment

00:11:55.930 --> 00:12:00.320
or where things are located
to help users make decisions.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:03.865
So if you're trying to
decide, have some resources,

00:12:03.865 --> 00:12:05.960
and where should I
concentrate those resources--

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:08.980
I want to find out
where are they needed--

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:10.480
you want to be able
to look at a map

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:12.970
to find out where the
most action is in order

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:15.245
to deploy your resources
to that location.

00:12:15.245 --> 00:12:17.620
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Like a ride
share company, for example.

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:18.760
ANGELA YU: Exactly, yeah.

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:21.040
MARK MANDEL: Is this
limited to if I saw it--

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:23.290
I know I've seen Google
Maps embedded on a web page.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.030
Is it limited to
that kind of view,

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:27.835
or could I use the
data in other formats?

00:12:27.835 --> 00:12:29.710
How does this end up
being a user experience?

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:32.200
ANGELA YU: Displaying a map is
kind of the most popular way

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:33.190
to use our API.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:36.970
But we also have a very
rich database of places,

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:41.080
so that includes businesses,
but also just general places,

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.900
like parks or
government buildings.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.840
And all of that rich database
is available through the Places

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:48.050
API.

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:49.900
And then there's
also the Routes API

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:52.330
that gives you access to
things like the directions,

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:54.220
whether through driving
or walking or biking,

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:56.218
and transit and roads.

00:12:56.218 --> 00:12:57.760
So you are that ride
sharing company,

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:00.700
and you're seeing a geolocation
of one of your drivers,

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:02.900
and they're nebulously
off of a road.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:05.800
You can snap it to a real
road that they must be on.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:08.680
And so all of those APIs are
available through the Maps

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:09.430
Platform.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:10.390
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So there's location.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:11.560
There's kind of like
how you actually

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:13.310
do routing, and then
moving some things around.

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:15.160
I think we've actually seen
some pretty interesting use

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:16.960
cases recently of
games implementing

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:19.310
this to create kind of an
augmented reality experience

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:19.580
as well.

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:20.205
ANGELA YU: Mhm.

00:13:20.205 --> 00:13:22.180
Yeah, real world
gaming is a big trend.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:24.880
It was really popularized
by "Pokémon GO."

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:30.010
And from that, we have expanded
our API to game developers

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:32.440
to enable real world gaming
where there is a Playable

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:36.550
Locations API, where game
developers can identify where

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:40.030
are good places for people to
have real world interactions

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:41.110
with things in the game.

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:45.490
And last year, there were five
games released using that API.

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:47.240
And there will be a
lot more this year.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:49.698
But one of the most successful
ones is like "Walking Dead."

00:13:49.698 --> 00:13:53.770
So wherever you are, you can
see zombie attacks coming.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:56.230
And you might have to
collaborate with other players

00:13:56.230 --> 00:13:58.060
in order to defeat the zombies.

00:13:58.060 --> 00:13:59.860
So these are really
fun things that you

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:02.530
can do to bring that digital
experience and the real world

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:03.430
experience together.

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:06.220
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Can we make
sure there's a real zombie API

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:06.770
just in case?

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:08.020
Just in case that does happen.

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:09.250
MARK MANDEL: Just in case.

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:09.880
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
It'd be nice to have

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:11.470
a map saying, hey, go here.

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:12.190
Don't go here.

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:13.370
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:15.370
You want your zombie API
as part of the Maps API

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:16.720
that tracks all the zombies.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:17.400
ANGELA YU: Yes.

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.150
I will definitely
prioritize that feature.

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:19.510
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Thank you.

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:20.260
Appreciate that.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:21.010
Thank you so much.

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:22.510
MARK MANDEL: And it sounds like
there's-- so there's a couple

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:23.530
of games that have
been using it.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:24.970
What other kind
of trends have you

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:27.670
been seeing of people trying
to adopt this and maybe create

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:29.073
some new experiences?

00:14:29.073 --> 00:14:29.740
ANGELA YU: Yeah.

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:32.950
We say the problem of showing
a map and dropping a pin on it

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:34.327
has been solved for 10 years.

00:14:34.327 --> 00:14:36.910
So we're really looking at what
are the new things that people

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:38.170
are doing with it.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:40.000
Obviously, mobile
has been bringing

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:43.030
a lot of interesting
use cases to how

00:14:43.030 --> 00:14:44.560
people use the Maps Platform.

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:48.190
Because geolocation
around mobile device

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:50.080
is kind of critical
information for anyone

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:51.320
that's moving around.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:54.640
Beyond that, I come from,
actually, an IoT background,

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:55.710
Internet of Things.

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:57.490
And this is the idea
of connected things.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:00.520
Not just phones and
computers, but any asset

00:15:00.520 --> 00:15:02.150
could be connected
to the internet.

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:05.320
And especially when those things
are out in the physical world,

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:06.730
they all have location.

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:08.500
And it's very
important to whoever

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:11.960
cares to connect to that
thing to know where that is.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:14.800
So we've seen a lot
of organizations,

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:18.940
both from the enterprise
to consumer realm, be

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:21.790
interested in collecting
data off of these things,

00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:25.870
mapping them so that they can
show the status of things out

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:26.560
in the world.

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:28.780
And maybe many
things in one area

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:32.320
are all showing the same
state or are trending one way.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:34.690
And then they can make better
decisions based on that.

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:38.980
One example that I saw recently
was one of our Google Developer

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:39.580
experts.

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:41.590
These are external
developers that

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:44.270
have used our APIs extensively.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:47.410
He has built a
prototype of tracking

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:50.840
bike-sharing bikes and
where they're moving around.

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:53.530
And he's feeding it
to a Firebase database

00:15:53.530 --> 00:15:55.750
and then pulling that
database information,

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:57.460
displaying it on a Google Map.

00:15:57.460 --> 00:15:59.860
And that really
shows the hotspots

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:02.140
of where these bikes
are actually being used.

00:16:02.140 --> 00:16:03.640
So then a bike-sharing
company could

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:06.520
use that data and better
deploy where their bikes

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:07.740
should be available.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:11.050
Because the worst thing is
if they were to put them off

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:13.550
in where they think the
biking locations are,

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:16.600
and nobody actually is
trying to use it from there.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:18.820
That's a really
interesting project

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:20.720
that he open sourced it.

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:22.270
And it's out there on Hackster.

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:24.540
We can put a link to
it in the show notes.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:26.890
But I think that's a
really great example

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:29.565
of using IoT data,
displaying it on a map

00:16:29.565 --> 00:16:31.690
and being able to make a
lot better decisions based

00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:32.660
on that data.

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:33.880
MARK MANDEL: I think you brought
up something interesting there,

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:36.172
too, where you were talking
about Firebase and the Maps

00:16:36.172 --> 00:16:37.180
API.

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:39.670
Given that we're the "Google
Cloud Platform" podcast,

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:41.680
are you seeing
interesting interactions

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:44.920
or grouping of technology with
Google Cloud with the Maps API

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:46.570
mishmashing together?

00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:47.660
How does that look to you?

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:48.327
ANGELA YU: Yeah.

00:16:48.327 --> 00:16:50.010
One of the biggest
trends that we see

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:51.857
is the big data visualization.

00:16:51.857 --> 00:16:53.940
And we have a really great
Code Lab that shows you

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:56.910
how to query big
data from the query

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:58.590
and bring that
into a Google Map.

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:01.920
So then any data
that you have that

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:05.609
has a location aspect to it
can then be put on a map.

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:08.670
And you can process
really huge data sets,

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:11.760
and be able to visualize them,
and really communicate and tell

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.760
stories about what is
important in that data,

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:17.250
that you just couldn't
really surface before.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:20.670
One of the really interesting
things in big data,

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:22.619
there's the BigQuery
public data sets.

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:24.119
If you don't have
your own data set,

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:25.786
you can play with
these public data sets

00:17:25.786 --> 00:17:27.180
and do really
interesting things,

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:29.520
just to practice
that muscle and see

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:32.460
what you can do in terms of
data visualizations of location

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:33.480
based data.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:37.620
But there is also an open
source library called deck.gl

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:40.950
that lets you build really
beautiful data visualizations.

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:43.620
They have all these layers that
you can put over a Google Map--

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:44.280
MARK MANDEL: Oh, interesting.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:45.780
ANGELA YU: --that
are specialized

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:47.490
for different kinds of data.

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:49.800
If you had the roots of--

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.080
one of the public data
sets is the New York City

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:53.840
taxi and limousine services.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:56.910
And all of the trips that
the taxis have been taking,

00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:00.855
you can use their trips layer
to actually draw on a map

00:18:00.855 --> 00:18:03.690
the kind of like time
lapse view of the trips

00:18:03.690 --> 00:18:06.660
that all of these taxis were
taking in the time period.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:08.670
It just works really
well, and it handles

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:10.210
huge data sets really well.

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:12.780
So that's one of the things
that we announced at I/O,

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:15.120
was integration with
deck.gl that we're

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:16.920
really excited for
our developers to try.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:17.670
MARK MANDEL: Neat.

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:18.210
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Very cool.

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:20.785
Were there any other kind of
cool announcements from I/O?

00:18:20.785 --> 00:18:22.410
ANGELA YU: From the
Maps Platform side,

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:26.730
we have a new SDK for Android
that we've put out for beta.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:30.000
And so we're really looking
for developers to try it out,

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.552
give us feedback before
we release the GA version.

00:18:32.552 --> 00:18:35.010
MARK MANDEL: What makes this
particular version for Android

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:35.910
particularly special?

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:38.610
ANGELA YU: We have actually
brought it out standalone

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:42.360
from the Google mobile
core, so that we

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:44.040
can release more frequently.

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:46.860
Basically, that takes us
out of their release cycle

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:49.710
and puts us on a more
frequent release cycle.

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:53.352
And there are a lot of rendering
improvements for performance.

00:18:53.352 --> 00:18:55.560
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So with
all those kind of use cases

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.240
you were just talking about
and how a lot of people

00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:58.890
can get their hands
it, what would you

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:01.450
say is the coolest
experience you've seen

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:02.700
built with Google Maps, right?

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:04.608
The visualization
sounds pretty awesome.

00:19:04.608 --> 00:19:06.150
But think about
what's the next step.

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:06.990
MARK MANDEL: I mean,
we had zombies.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:08.040
What's better than zombies?

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:09.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I don't
know if we can top zombies.

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:10.830
ANGELA YU: Yeah.

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:13.157
I think there are a lot
of really complex things

00:19:13.157 --> 00:19:14.490
that are coming up in the world.

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:18.520
And now it's possible to process
these really complex decisions.

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:20.790
So I'm really
excited to see what's

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:23.970
possible with-- ridesharing
companies are really doing

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:25.470
very complex routing with us.

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:28.320
Because they not only have to
go from point A to point B,

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:30.210
but consider all these
other stops in between

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:31.890
and optimizations.

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:35.550
And what I am most passionate
about is augmented reality

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:39.360
and seeing that go
beyond just the gaming

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.140
applications about
augmented reality,

00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:47.100
but also how we see the data on
the world around us overlaid.

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:51.150
So one of the big things in
the Android Google Maps app

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:53.670
is the augmented reality
walking directions,

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:55.480
which really helps
people navigate,

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:57.390
especially in big
cities, when they're

00:19:57.390 --> 00:19:58.680
trying to orient themselves.

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:01.140
And augmented reality
arrows that kind of

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:04.260
show you exactly where to
walk, those are very helpful.

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:06.540
And I see a lot of more
applications coming out

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:09.030
for making those kinds of
experiences, of showing you

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:10.980
what you're looking
for actually overlaid

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:12.190
on your physical world.

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:16.770
If you wanted to know kind of
the weird stuff that we see,

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:18.300
really whimsical things.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:21.030
And one of the most
whimsical things that kind of

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:23.070
stood the test of
time is this idea

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:24.780
of making an earth sandwich.

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:26.613
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What
is an earth sandwich?

00:20:26.613 --> 00:20:28.020
Besides delicious, I'm sure.

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:31.680
ANGELA YU: So it's kind of like
the idea that every kid has of,

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:34.770
if I dig a tunnel all the way
to the other side of the world,

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:35.603
where will I end up?

00:20:35.603 --> 00:20:36.645
MARK MANDEL: Burnt alive.

00:20:36.645 --> 00:20:37.260
[SIZZLING]

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:41.640
ANGELA YU: That idea of you
being on one side of the world

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:44.410
and what is on the other side
of this sphere, this globe--

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:44.692
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:20:44.692 --> 00:20:45.192
Got it.

00:20:45.192 --> 00:20:48.730
ANGELA YU: Those two points on
the globe are called antipodes.

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:52.260
And there are map tools
that let you find antipodes

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:53.670
of from where you are.

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:54.610
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:56.910
ANGELA YU: And most of
the landmass, actually,

00:20:56.910 --> 00:20:59.240
the antipode is in the water.

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:01.050
So there is actually
only, I think,

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:06.830
4% the world that you could
have a land-to-land sandwich.

00:21:06.830 --> 00:21:10.920
And so these maps were created
to help people find antipodes--

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:14.100
New Zealand has an
antipode in Spain--

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:18.030
for people to partner up
and try to synchronize, so

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:21.300
that they can put a piece
of bread on one side,

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:23.710
and a piece of bread
on the other side.

00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:25.425
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh, and that's
where the sandwich comes in.

00:21:25.425 --> 00:21:26.205
MARK MANDEL: Oh,
that's fantastic.

00:21:26.205 --> 00:21:27.480
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Oh, that's brilliant.

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:28.230
ANGELA YU: Yeah,
and so there are

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:30.330
videos where people
document themselves putting

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:32.370
a piece of bread on
this specific latitude

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:35.550
and longitude and their partner
doing it on the other side.

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:38.822
And they have successfully
created an earth sandwich.

00:21:38.822 --> 00:21:40.780
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh,
which probably would be--

00:21:40.780 --> 00:21:42.750
there's going to be a lot of
disappointed six-year-olds out

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:44.470
there that hear that they
can't-- they'll probably end up

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:45.343
with water, though.

00:21:45.343 --> 00:21:46.010
ANGELA YU: Yeah.

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:49.653
MARK MANDEL: Some galactic
entity is just like, mm, good.

00:21:49.653 --> 00:21:50.320
ANGELA YU: Yeah.

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:53.800
Actually, with students, one
of the most popular tools

00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:56.680
in the classroom is this
website called The True Size.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:59.380
Because if you are
a math nerd, you

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:01.540
know that the maps
we're used to seeing

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:04.960
are very distorted to how
the countries actually

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:07.730
lay out on the globe surface.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:10.840
And so we are so used to
seeing these flat maps

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:13.210
that our ideas of
the size of countries

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:14.770
gets really distorted.

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:17.200
And there's this
interactive web page

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:19.900
called The True
Size, where you can

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:24.670
drag a country to the equator or
overlay it on any other country

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:28.390
to compare their sizes and see
how actually large or small

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:28.960
they are.

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:29.890
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
What was your biggest

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:30.760
surprise when you tried this?

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:32.177
ANGELA YU: One of
my big surprises

00:22:32.177 --> 00:22:34.300
was I didn't realize
that Mexico is actually

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:36.700
as tall as the contiguous US.

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:40.270
And Canada and Russia
look really huge.

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:42.520
But actually, when you bring
them down to the equator,

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:43.970
they're much smaller.

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:46.240
I think Russia is still
really big compared

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:48.970
to other countries, but
it's not as humongous

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:50.650
as it looks on the flat map.

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:50.800
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:51.950
I mean, there was an update.

00:22:51.950 --> 00:22:52.810
I don't remember when it was.

00:22:52.810 --> 00:22:55.060
But there was an update to
Google Maps, the interface,

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:56.490
where if you zoom
out far enough,

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:58.990
it's switched from that kind
of standard Mercator projection

00:22:58.990 --> 00:23:00.630
into the globe one, right?

00:23:00.630 --> 00:23:01.505
ANGELA YU: Mhm, yeah.

00:23:01.505 --> 00:23:03.730
That is just like it just
switches to that satellite

00:23:03.730 --> 00:23:04.300
view.

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:06.940
And it just goes to that
really true representation

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:07.767
of the world.

00:23:07.767 --> 00:23:09.850
It's funny that you mentioned
Mercator projection.

00:23:09.850 --> 00:23:13.600
Because there's an xkcd
comic that actually

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:16.540
types you based on your
favorite map projection.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.130
And if Mercator
is your favorite,

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:21.137
then you're kind of
just like a lemming

00:23:21.137 --> 00:23:23.470
that goes along with whatever's
fed to you in the world.

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:24.928
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well, I was going

00:23:24.928 --> 00:23:27.790
to say I love the Mercator
projection for its nice, flat,

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:28.865
and even distribution.

00:23:28.865 --> 00:23:29.740
But I guess I'm not--

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:30.300
I'm just wrong.

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:31.900
MARK MANDEL: I'm impressed you
know what the word Mercator is

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.160
because I have no idea.

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:35.990
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I took a
geology class 15 years ago.

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:36.760
I know things.

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:37.770
ANGELA YU: You've
paid attention.

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:39.130
MARK MANDEL: Oh, actually,
interesting question then.

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:40.840
So we were having an
initial conversation

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:43.420
before we started about
all the different latitude

00:23:43.420 --> 00:23:46.100
and longitude standards or
how you track maps and stuff.

00:23:46.100 --> 00:23:48.650
If I'm using the
Maps API, do I need

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:50.470
to go take a geology
class or something?

00:23:50.470 --> 00:23:52.360
Like do I need to know
geography or whatever

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:52.900
it is that I need to be doing.

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:53.690
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I
do not recommend it.

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:55.090
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
do I need to do that?

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:55.885
Or does it make it simpler?

00:23:55.885 --> 00:23:56.960
Or how does that work?

00:23:56.960 --> 00:23:58.543
ANGELA YU: I think
we've tried to make

00:23:58.543 --> 00:24:00.010
it as simple as possible.

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:01.600
The latitude and
longitude, I think

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:03.940
you might be used to
seeing it expressed

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:07.060
in minutes and seconds
in this sexagesimal--

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:08.140
[GUITAR CHORDS]

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:08.780
--way.

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:12.430
But when you actually use
Google Maps on your computer

00:24:12.430 --> 00:24:14.860
or on your phone, you'll
see latitude and longitude

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:16.420
expressed in a decimal way.

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:20.320
So it's still going to be
the same first two or three

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:23.770
numbers, like 122 degrees West.

00:24:23.770 --> 00:24:27.370
And then instead of having
to convert in your mind

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:30.068
to minutes and seconds,
it's actually just decimal.

00:24:30.068 --> 00:24:32.110
MARK MANDEL: I just do
that conversion in my mind

00:24:32.110 --> 00:24:32.833
all the time.

00:24:32.833 --> 00:24:34.000
It's just what I do for fun.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:35.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI: The formula's
written on the back your hand.

00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:35.970
It's fine.

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:36.850
MARK MANDEL: Oh,
no, it's in my head.

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:37.340
Yeah.

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:38.215
No, that makes sense.

00:24:38.215 --> 00:24:40.930
And then, too, if I'm like,
all this sounds awesome,

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:42.910
and I want to get
started, where do I begin?

00:24:42.910 --> 00:24:43.660
Where should I go?

00:24:43.660 --> 00:24:45.243
ANGELA YU: One of
the things that I've

00:24:45.243 --> 00:24:47.170
been really happy about
since I've started

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:49.150
is every time I talk to
an external developer,

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:50.920
they tell me the
documentation is great.

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:53.210
So I'd start with
our documentation.

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:55.990
We have a ton of
tutorials on there.

00:24:55.990 --> 00:24:58.480
We have from the
most simple just show

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:02.890
a map to actually call a
complex distance matrix

00:25:02.890 --> 00:25:05.590
to figure out all of
the different times

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:08.650
and distances of multiple routes
to get from point A to point B.

00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:10.930
And all of that is
provided in tutorials.

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:12.490
We have code samples.

00:25:12.490 --> 00:25:16.290
We have Code Labs to teach you
some of the most common tasks.

00:25:16.290 --> 00:25:17.860
And all of that is out there.

00:25:17.860 --> 00:25:20.770
On GitHub, we have a
Google Maps organization

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:23.890
that has a bunch of repos for
client libraries and utility

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:24.940
libraries.

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:27.670
So on the regular
Maps Platform, we

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:32.450
have the JavaScript API,
Android SDK, and iOS SDK.

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:34.210
And we have a
bunch of REST APIs.

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:36.340
But in our GitHub
organization, we

00:25:36.340 --> 00:25:41.890
have client libraries for
Java, for Node, and Go, Python,

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:44.758
and kind of almost anything that
you might want to program in.

00:25:44.758 --> 00:25:47.050
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And so the
process for someone to get

00:25:47.050 --> 00:25:48.760
started, just like
any other API,

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:51.565
they can just go to the GCP
console, enable the Maps API,

00:25:51.565 --> 00:25:54.190
get the key, and then start with
any of those tutorials, right?

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:54.857
ANGELA YU: Yeah.

00:25:54.857 --> 00:25:56.500
And one of the great
things about us

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:59.710
is that we, on top of any
credits that you've already

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:01.600
gotten through Google
Cloud Platform,

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:04.420
you also get a
$200 monthly credit

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:07.150
recurring for the Maps APIs.

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:11.330
So your first $200 of Maps
API spend is free every month.

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:15.100
And so that covers any kind of
experimentation or development

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:17.080
that you want to do,
to just try things out

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:19.690
before you actually
go into production

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:21.230
and get a lot of users.

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:24.880
So with that, we try
to encourage and enable

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:27.460
more of that experimentation
and playing around

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:29.110
before it starts to
cost you anything.

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:29.800
MARK MANDEL: That sounds great.

00:26:29.800 --> 00:26:31.250
And it sounds like
so that's being

00:26:31.250 --> 00:26:32.500
charged as a separate product.

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:34.380
Is that billing
unified with the cloud?

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:36.280
Do I need to set up a separate
account or anything like that?

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:36.460
ANGELA YU: No.

00:26:36.460 --> 00:26:38.500
It's all built into your
Google Cloud account.

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:40.570
For any project that
you set up, you just

00:26:40.570 --> 00:26:42.850
want to go into the API
libraries or marketplace

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:46.008
and enable the Maps APIs
that you want to use.

00:26:46.008 --> 00:26:47.800
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
is there anything else

00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:49.925
that a developer would need
to know to get started?

00:26:49.925 --> 00:26:52.623
ANGELA YU: It's really
self-explanatory.

00:26:52.623 --> 00:26:54.540
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That's
a good claim to make.

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:55.683
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:26:55.683 --> 00:26:56.350
ANGELA YU: Yeah.

00:26:56.350 --> 00:26:58.450
We have a really great
technical writing team.

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:01.690
We invest a lot in keeping
our documentation up to date.

00:27:01.690 --> 00:27:04.390
And I personally
am really invested

00:27:04.390 --> 00:27:06.760
in keeping our samples updated.

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:10.120
And all of our libraries
and samples on GitHub

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:12.130
are open source, so
we really encourage

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:15.190
the community to submit
pull requests, open issues,

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:17.860
let us know if things
are not working for them.

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:20.590
Or if they have ideas for
ways that our libraries could

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:22.630
perform better, have at it.

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:25.730
MARK MANDEL: What's a demo
you wish someone would build?

00:27:25.730 --> 00:27:26.800
ANGELA YU: Hm.

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:29.590
One of my pet projects is
with augmented reality,

00:27:29.590 --> 00:27:33.940
showing names and ratings
on buildings overlaid right

00:27:33.940 --> 00:27:34.630
around you.

00:27:34.630 --> 00:27:37.660
Because whenever I travel
to a foreign country,

00:27:37.660 --> 00:27:39.940
and it might be harder
for me to understand

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:41.950
or it's a very
dense place, it can

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:45.700
be really hard to match up
an address to a building that

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:46.838
has no numbers on it.

00:27:46.838 --> 00:27:47.630
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:50.890
ANGELA YU: So having that kind
of translation and overlay

00:27:50.890 --> 00:27:53.410
to help me kind of
figure out where to eat

00:27:53.410 --> 00:27:55.570
or where is this place
