00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:15.400
So what is Helm?

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:16.420
Why is that important?

00:31:16.420 --> 00:31:17.440
MARK MANDEL: OK, so yes.

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:18.780
So Helm is an open
source project.

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:20.500
It's been around for
quite awhile, actually.

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:22.150
We're going to talk
primarily about Helm 2,

00:31:22.150 --> 00:31:24.025
and we'll talk a little
bit about that later.

00:31:24.025 --> 00:31:26.410
But it is worth noting, we
do have an episode way back

00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:28.180
in the day, episode
50, talking about Helm

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:30.640
with Michelle Noorali
and Matthew Butcher,

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:32.110
talking about Helm in depth.

00:31:32.110 --> 00:31:35.435
But at its base level, it's a
package manager for Kubernetes.

00:31:35.435 --> 00:31:36.560
OK, so what does that mean?

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:38.143
So the thing I like
to talk about when

00:31:38.143 --> 00:31:41.470
I talk about Helm is like if
you've ever used APT on Linux,

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:44.650
or yum, or Homebrew, or anything
like that on a Mac device,

00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:47.620
or a Chocolatey on Windows,
basically it's like,

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:50.650
how do I install x thing,
plus all the dependencies

00:31:50.650 --> 00:31:51.580
that it has as well?

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:53.710
Because sometimes
things are complicated,

00:31:53.710 --> 00:31:55.040
and they have lots of stuff.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:57.110
And so Helm's really nice
in that first of all,

00:31:57.110 --> 00:31:58.870
it has a series
of what they call

00:31:58.870 --> 00:32:01.760
stable charts and the chart
is the package, basically.

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:04.660
So if you're like, I want to
install multi cluster Redis,

00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:07.653
for example, I can go look
for the Redis Helm chart.

00:32:07.653 --> 00:32:09.070
There are some
configurable values

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:10.000
that I can push through there.

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:12.042
And it's going to go and
create all the YAML that

00:32:12.042 --> 00:32:14.130
needs to be created to
basically specify, OK,

00:32:14.130 --> 00:32:15.880
this is how the Redis
configuration should

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:16.450
see set up.

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:18.820
You've specified that you
want this many nodes in it.

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:19.960
And we can set it all up.

00:32:19.960 --> 00:32:21.850
And all the dependencies are
all set up, and it's good to go.

00:32:21.850 --> 00:32:23.140
So like, sweet, awesome.

00:32:23.140 --> 00:32:24.040
And that works well.

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:26.317
The next level of
that is also, how

00:32:26.317 --> 00:32:28.900
do I either delete that, say I
don't want it anymore, and make

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:29.730
sure it cleans everything up?

00:32:29.730 --> 00:32:31.030
But also, how do I upgrade it?

00:32:31.030 --> 00:32:32.170
So maybe there's a new
version of the chart.

00:32:32.170 --> 00:32:33.460
There's a new version
of Redis coming out.

00:32:33.460 --> 00:32:34.910
I want to do
something like that.

00:32:34.910 --> 00:32:36.520
Now suddenly I can
do a Helm upgrade.

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:38.920
And it'll do all the nice
dependency changes and all

00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:39.970
the things I need
to change that way,

00:32:39.970 --> 00:32:42.340
so that I can change that
platform as I need to change

00:32:42.340 --> 00:32:43.550
it, which is really nice.

00:32:43.550 --> 00:32:45.910
The third version of
that is that you might

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:47.710
have an application yourself.

00:32:47.710 --> 00:32:50.245
And you might be like, oh,
cool, I have this application.

00:32:50.245 --> 00:32:51.620
And it's dependent
on maybe Redis

00:32:51.620 --> 00:32:53.200
and maybe several other things.

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:55.450
So I can set up a
chart with sub charts

00:32:55.450 --> 00:32:57.367
and have dependencies
set up that way as well.

00:32:57.367 --> 00:32:59.825
Maybe there's some configurable
values, that kind of stuff.

00:32:59.825 --> 00:33:02.360
So then I can create a chart
specific for my application.

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:03.858
I could even publish
that publicly

00:33:03.858 --> 00:33:06.400
if I wanted to as well, so other
people could take advantage.

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:08.733
You can actually have multiple
repositories within Helm,

00:33:08.733 --> 00:33:10.750
so if you want a publisher
in charts, you can,

00:33:10.750 --> 00:33:12.280
either internally or publicly.

00:33:12.280 --> 00:33:15.670
And so it handles that sort
of create, install, upgrade,

00:33:15.670 --> 00:33:19.012
delete lifecycle of complex
Kubernetes applications.

00:33:19.012 --> 00:33:19.720
It's pretty nice.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:20.590
I quite like it.

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:21.840
We have one for the
project I work on,

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:22.882
which it works on Agones.

00:33:22.882 --> 00:33:24.870
It tracks when did
new versions come up,

00:33:24.870 --> 00:33:26.620
what version is kind
of live, what version

00:33:26.620 --> 00:33:28.000
is available in the repo--

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:28.967
pretty comprehensive.

00:33:28.967 --> 00:33:29.800
I should make note--

00:33:29.800 --> 00:33:31.550
Helm 3 is coming up.

00:33:31.550 --> 00:33:33.598
So Helm 3 is actually
a full rewrite of Helm.

00:33:33.598 --> 00:33:34.640
You can still use Helm 2.

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:36.432
Helm 2 is great, and
stable, and wonderful.

00:33:36.432 --> 00:33:38.818
They're getting rid
of some dependencies

00:33:38.818 --> 00:33:40.360
inside the Kubernetes
cluster itself.

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:41.660
They have a thing
called Tiller usually

00:33:41.660 --> 00:33:42.340
that they used to run in there.

00:33:42.340 --> 00:33:44.470
They're actually taking
full advantage of CRDs.

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:45.430
If I don't get
any of this wrong,

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:46.780
you can actually check all
this out on the blog post.

00:33:46.780 --> 00:33:48.720
It's going to be a much more
lightweight installation.

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:49.928
It's going to be really nice.

00:33:49.928 --> 00:33:52.002
But that's been developed
fully in the open.

00:33:52.002 --> 00:33:53.460
You can check it
all out at GitHub.

00:33:53.460 --> 00:33:54.850
They were like
writing design docs

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:55.790
before they write any code.

00:33:55.790 --> 00:33:57.123
It's actually super, super cool.

00:33:57.123 --> 00:34:01.132
But the 3.0.0
alpha-1 is available

00:34:01.132 --> 00:34:02.590
if you want to muck
around with it.

00:34:02.590 --> 00:34:04.522
But yeah, Helm's really nice.

00:34:04.522 --> 00:34:05.980
So just checking
it out, it's worth

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:07.540
having a look at if you
have a complex Kubernetes

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:09.670
application you want to deploy,
and you're looking for a way

00:34:09.670 --> 00:34:10.690
to package that up nicely.

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:11.949
Does that answer
your question, Mark?

00:34:11.949 --> 00:34:12.489
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I think so.

00:34:12.489 --> 00:34:14.350
So let me read that back
to you if that's all right.

00:34:14.350 --> 00:34:16.580
I mean, basically, you're able
to kind of pull down a chart

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:18.955
if you want to take some kind
of a service, for instance.

00:34:18.955 --> 00:34:21.005
Like for instance, you
gave an example of Redis.

00:34:21.005 --> 00:34:22.630
And say I want this
in memory database,

00:34:22.630 --> 00:34:24.889
so you pull down the chart,
and you kind of deploy it.

00:34:24.889 --> 00:34:26.350
And of course, if updates
get rolled out of that,

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:27.889
like a normal package
manager, you can say,

00:34:27.889 --> 00:34:29.139
oh, there's updates available.

00:34:29.139 --> 00:34:30.520
So I'm going to go grab those.

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:33.103
Helm takes care of all the work
there and helps deploy it out.

00:34:33.103 --> 00:34:35.909
But of course, applications are
usually more than one service.

00:34:35.909 --> 00:34:36.409
Right?

00:34:36.409 --> 00:34:37.540
They might be really complex.

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:38.670
They might have dependencies.

00:34:38.670 --> 00:34:40.330
And so you kind of
build all that out.

00:34:40.330 --> 00:34:42.670
Plus, you're able to kind of
take your own application,

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:45.340
maybe build it up with a
chart that has sub charts,

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:48.320
and then push that out there,
if, for instance, like Agones,

00:34:48.320 --> 00:34:49.842
you kind of have
this complex tree

00:34:49.842 --> 00:34:51.550
that you need in order
to deploy the app.

00:34:51.550 --> 00:34:51.940
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:53.690
Agones is probably a
good example as well.

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:56.679
I mean, we have a variety of
services, some deployments,

00:34:56.679 --> 00:34:58.607
a bunch of RBAC controls
in there as well,

00:34:58.607 --> 00:34:59.690
and a whole bunch of CRDs.

00:34:59.690 --> 00:35:01.568
Yeah, there's those
and things like that.

00:35:01.568 --> 00:35:02.860
And so that becomes super nice.

00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:05.390
And Helm does a really nice
job, as well, of templating.

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:06.670
So if you want to
have includes, or you

00:35:06.670 --> 00:35:08.337
want to do loops
around stuff, basically

00:35:08.337 --> 00:35:10.940
being able to generate that
YAML file sort of on the fly,

00:35:10.940 --> 00:35:12.010
there's a lot of
nice tooling in there

00:35:12.010 --> 00:35:13.120
that allows you to do that, too.

00:35:13.120 --> 00:35:14.620
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it sounds
like a super cool concept.

00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:16.010
If people are getting
started with Kubernetes,

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:18.187
it might be a great way
to get an application,

00:35:18.187 --> 00:35:20.020
or set of services, or
a set of deployments,

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:21.160
or a combination
of all those kind

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:22.540
of up and running
pretty quickly.

